      "text": "To find the smallest possible degree of a nonzero polynomial with rational coefficients that has all of the numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots, we must consider the properties of polynomials with rational coefficients. \n\nA polynomial with rational coefficients has roots that are either rational or pairs of the form \\(a + \\sqrt{b}\\) where \\(a\\) is rational and \\(b\\) is a non-negative integer, and those roots must occur in conjugate pairs. Therefore, each irrational root \\(a + \\sqrt{b}\\) requires its conjugate \\(a - \\sqrt{b}\\) to be a root of the polynomial.\n\nGiven the roots \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, \\; 2+\\sqrt{3}, \\; 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\; \\dots, \\; 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\), for each of these roots \\(a + \\sqrt{b}\\), we need its conjugate \\(a - \\sqrt{b}\\) to also be a root. \n\nThe number of such conjugate pairs is equal to the number of roots above, which is 1000. Each pair of roots \\(a + \\sqrt{b}\\) and \\(a - \\sqrt{b}\\) contributes 2 roots to the polynomial. Therefore, the total number of roots is \\(1000 \\times 2 = 2000\\).\n\nThus, the smallest possible degree of the polynomial is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2000}\n\\]",
